WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.640
The tink is gone.

2
00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:05.100
Then a business customer plugs in a fax
machine, and it won't go through.

3
00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:05.840
Not once.

4
00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.360
Its tones run from one thousand

5
00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.940
to eighteen hundred hertz,
and every one gets out loud enough.

6
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:13.090
The fax sends each page
in short tone bursts,

7
00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:16.290
and every tone must arrive within
one and a half samples of every other.

8
00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:17.410
That's its window.

9
00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:20.570
At the far end, each burst near
eighteen hundred hertz comes three samples

10
00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:22.210
late, and slides into the next one.

11
00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:23.210
That's the smear.

12
00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.500
Lesson three said a line
does two things to each tone.

13
00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.120
It scales it, and it delays it.

14
00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.380
Nobody checked the delay.

15
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.600
On the wheel,
the output's dot trails the input's.

16
00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:32.640
That's the phase.

17
00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:35.480
A steady tone is late by its phase
over its turn per sample.

18
00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:37.770
Take a pure delay of three samples.

19
00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.630
Five hundred hertz trails by sixty-seven
and a half degrees,

20
00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.090
one thousand by one hundred thirty-five,

21
00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:43.750
two thousand by two hundred seventy.

22
00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.710
Double the tone, double the phase.

23
00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:47.390
Every tone, three samples late.

24
00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:48.610
That's linear phase.

25
00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.160
Your notch's phase creeps along,

26
00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:52.780
then bends up near two thousand hertz.

27
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.220
One thousand hertz
is point two of a sample late.

28
00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:56.840
Eighteen hundred is point five six.

29
00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:58.120
A third of a sample apart.

30
00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.400
Could that wreck a fax?

31
00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:02.890
Send one burst at eighteen hundred hertz.

32
00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:04.850
It comes out three point two four samples

33
00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:05.150
late.

34
00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:06.110
More than three.

35
00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:08.370
The wiggle inside is only
half a sample behind,

36
00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:10.490
but the shape of the burst
arrives much later.

37
00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:11.850
And a burst isn't one tone.

38
00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:13.230
It's many neighboring tones.

39
00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:15.190
Take the smallest burst,

40
00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:16.490
seventeen hundred and eighteen hundred

41
00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:17.630
hertz together.

42
00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:19.930
When their dots line up, the sum swells.

43
00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.710
The faster dot gains
four and a half degrees per sample.

44
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:23.670
Through the notch,

45
00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:26.610
eighteen hundred is set back
eleven point six degrees more,

46
00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.310
and must catch that up first.

47
00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.330
The swell is two point five eight samples

48
00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:30.810
late.

49
00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:34.090
That's a difference in phase
over a difference in turn.

50
00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:35.570
Slide the two tones together,

51
00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.630
and it becomes how steeply
the phase climbs.

52
00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:38.830
That's the group delay.

53
00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:41.310
Point three one samples
near one thousand hertz,

54
00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:42.530
three point two four near

55
00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:44.010
eighteen hundred. Three apart.

56
00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:45.690
The fax allows one and a half.

57
00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.230
So add a filter that only delays.

58
00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:51.630
A pole inside the circle, radius
point six five, at twelve hundred hertz.

59
00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:52.970
A zero on the same line,

60
00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:54.710
outside, at one over point six five.

61
00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:57.610
From any tone, the zero is always
one point five four times

62
00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:58.470
as far as the pole.

63
00:01:58.490 --> 00:01:59.790
That ratio never changes,

64
00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:01.150
so compared with zero hertz,

65
00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:02.830
every tone passes a hundred percent.

66
00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:03.690
That's an all-pass.

67
00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.880
It holds back the tones
near its pole the most.

68
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.260
Put that bump low in the band,
where the notch was early.

69
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.420
Now every tone arrives
between four point seven two

70
00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.560
and five point six seven samples late.

71
00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.400
Inside the window,
and not one gain changed. The price?

72
00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.020
Everyone waits five samples.

73
00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:20.710
Why not speed the late tones up?

74
00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.250
Running the all-pass backwards
puts a pole outside the circle,

75
00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:24.570
and every echo grows.

76
00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:26.410
You can't play a tone before it arrives.

77
00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:28.790
You can only make everyone else wait.
Next ticket.

78
00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:30.850
Three notches, multiplied into one rule.

79
00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:32.230
Perfect on the laptop.

80
00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:35.110
On a cheap eight-bit chip,
it hums, louder and louder.
